<attribute name="name" nodeId="7dd063fd">Establish application error handling approach</attribute>
<attribute name="guid" nodeId="2bafcc14">_9-1ykCpCEeWUXoxRhMgajw</attribute>
<attribute name="presentationName" nodeId="cb66cf67">Establish application error handling approach</attribute>
<attribute name="briefDescription" nodeId="92512ee7"></attribute>
<attribute name="suppressed" nodeId="c4b2f33b">false</attribute>
<attribute name="orderingGuide" nodeId="aff44195"></attribute>
<attribute name="variabilityType" nodeId="c7a903d1">na</attribute>
<attribute name="sectionName" nodeId="8ff8efc4"></attribute>
<attribute name="sectionDescription" nodeId="1b1f53f">&lt;p&gt;
    Define the design approach that should be followed for capturing, reporting and resolving errors within applications
    (e.g., interface and data transfer failures). The standards and guidelines should address the various categories of
    errors and define appropriate resolution paths for each.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    In order to capture, report and resolve package application errors:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Define error categories and types of errors within each category
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Define error conditions and resolution options
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Define error and process log
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Define reconciliation report (and distribution list)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Define post execution notification mechanism
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Define restart/recovery procedure
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Include monitoring/automated alert notifications where relevant and/or possible
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The error handling strategy is most commonly identified as relevant for interface scenarios to track failures of data
    transfers, but it is also relevant for other development (coding) scenarios. The development standards should also
    contain guidance on error handling for each development object. Those standards should align with the overall error
    handling strategy.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The error handling framework should categorize the errors (business, technical, miscellaneous) and provide resolution
    paths for the various categories:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Business errors include logic, data format, or data conversion errors
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Technical errors include communication (dropped database connections, unavailability of systems) or infrastructure
        errors (network availability or performance, client library availability, disk usage)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Miscellaneous errors include invalid logon credentials, scheduling, and time constraints or threshold errors
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
